{"title":"Topicwise Test-4 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 DA Calculus and Optimization-2","fileName":"067_Topicwise Test-4 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 DA Calculus and Optimization-2.html","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Data Science and AI","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":2700,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Given the function <img src=\"/media/0d261572eb385652947655806cd0323298c63d06.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> determine the Taylor expansion of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) around <em>x</em> = π upto the third non-zero term.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/ef32dafb0b9e1912183086bfe8eb2afb7ca513bf.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/273dbee3e75bba60cb9ad7a140109d4fe8b27655.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/51580c49166ba3561e2ab763202a4e31fefa0a85.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/636477efa02a06d136ae8d312932ce38dc094a09.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/61f270e834ecc521aa6530df0baf31e53718663e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 3<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – <em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 4<em>x</em>, determine the number of points where <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has either local maxima or minima is ________?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0","high":"0"},"solution":"<p>0</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f042fe2f581507c81b3d0caf797ced5d8838bc57.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The maximum value of <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em>(<em>x</em> – 1) (<em>x</em> – 2) in the interval [1, 2] is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> –2.42</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> –0.348</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/96b793cad0b8d30b489b6a86678d93ab05461a27.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Given <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) = ln x, suppose that second degree Taylor polynomial around <em>x</em> = 1 is used. If we approximate <em>f</em> (2) using this polynomial, which of the following is the closest result?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0.7</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.6</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.5</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0.9</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/25ace5d196169833c9af05a2283c6719bdd41cd4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5207e7b5a8af9da85e80ed6b25a2ca4cf80bcc30.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>A function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 1 – <em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + <em>x</em><sup>3</sup> is defined in the closed interval [–1, 1]. The value of <em>x</em> in the open interval (–1, 1) for which the mean value theorem is satisfied, is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/bddd140e09c8a4cd64f47961de50a1e2f42d6d50.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/002a8992b9b393e62dda6e9184a4b0707d1837e8.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/63c778cca27c050e57e21647d3084c7ab9ddc375.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4684343820e1e391290cb5976ab611b42779f3ae.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f2ade2b9ca5a81d5193e0ec62f321b099d974fa7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) be a continuous and differentiable function, over all real numbers.<br />\n<em>f</em> ′(<em>x</em>) is the first derivative of <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>).</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d6f6742ce1720f257781639260802ae7f24b05aa.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) has two local maxima and one inflection point.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) is non increasing function from [<em>A</em>, <em>C</em>].</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Point <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em> are real solution of <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>).</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/34819631af8dd8509a639ae984e2f4b7e2f1dfce.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e9d7422d633e6af3589daea545c74202af75d9ff.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the Taylor series <img src=\"/media/4eb7205ad48ba6895862009130ab437f5a399c78.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> centered at <em>z</em><sub>0</sub> = 2 Which of the following statements are true regarding the radius of convergence?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> If the Taylor series converges at z = 0 the radius of convergence <em>R</em> is at least 2.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> If the Taylor series converges at z = 3 the radius of convergence <em>R</em> is at least 1.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> It is possible for the Taylor series to converge at z = 0 and diverge at z = 3.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> If the Taylor series converges at z = 0, the series must converge at all points inside the disk of radius 2 around <em>z</em><sub>0</sub> = 2.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)<br />\n&bull; If the Taylor series converges at z = 0 the radius of convergence must be at least the distance<br />\nfrom z<sub>0</sub> = 2 to z = 0 which is 2. So, option (a) is true.<br />\n&bull; If the Taylor series converges at z = 3 the radius of convergence must be at least the distance<br />\nfrom z<sub>0</sub> = 2 to z = 3 which is 1. So, option (b) is true.<br />\n&bull; It is impossible for the Taylor series to converge at z = 0 and diverge at z = 3 If the series<br />\nconverges at z = 0 it will also converge at all points within the disk of radius 2, meaning it<br />\nwould converge at z = 3 as well. So, option (c) is false.<br />\n&bull; If the Taylor series converges at z = 0 it will converge at all points within the disk of radius 2<br />\naround z<sub>0</sub> = 2 meaning it will also converge at points inside this radius. So, option (d) is true.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em><sup>4 </sup>+ 4<em>x</em><sup>3</sup>, then</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Local maxima at <em>x</em> = 0</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Inflection point at <em>x</em> = –3</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Local minima at <em>x</em> = –3</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Local maxima at <em>x</em> = 0</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1240203897879780acfaaeeb4e6dc5988e72e182.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let a, b ∈ R – {0} be such that the function <img src=\"/media/6ddba6ec34d4e7c9ac0ec8867d698ed0972e9502.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> has extreme values at <em>x</em> = –1 and <em>x</em> = 2.<br />\n<strong>Statement 1:</strong> f has local maxima at <em>x</em> = –1 and local minima at <em>x</em> = 2.<br />\n<strong>Statement 2:</strong> a = 1 and b = <img src=\"/media/15c19c454a75cae8370cf5df71cc7f13a2f3a7d3.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Statement 1 is true statement 2 is false</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Both statements are true, statement 2 imply statement 1.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Both statements are true, statement 1 imply statement 2.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Statements 1 is false, statement 2 is true.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4af367b3164b77b52c94b0e3ea1177f1fea34117.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Suppose <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) is continuous and differentiable on the interval [–9, 2], <em>f</em>(–9) = –6 and <em>f</em>′(<em>x</em>) < 3.<br />\nWhich is the largest possible value for <em>f</em>(2)?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"26","high":"26"},"solution":"<p>26</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4b0486bc73f0452b82cf2683b93f2f744c3ee5f1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the mean value theorem <em>f</em>(<em>b</em>) – <em>f</em>(<em>a</em>) = (<em>b</em> – <em>a</em>) <em>f</em> ′(<em>c</em>) determining <em>c</em> lying between a and <em>b</em>, if <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em>(<em>x</em> – 1) (<em>x</em> – 2), <em>a</em> = 0 and <em>b</em> =<img src=\"/media/eb5d497b53d8928e635b95ea1c43419795e84c25.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> (Upto 2 decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.23","high":"0.23"},"solution":"<p>0.23 [0.22 - 0.24]</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8930d6e5356c8de007d57cc6cebfc36ff775cbfc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 3<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 7<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 5<em>x</em> + 6 is a real-valued function over the interval [0, 2].<br />\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>f</em>(x) has only one local maxima.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has two local maxima.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has only one local minima.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has two local minima.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8aa729d061f3dd492d432c1b7aa7228889954db0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>A function is defined by <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 3<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> – 12<em>x</em> + 5 for –2 ≤ <em>x</em> ≤ 3. Which one of the following statements is true about this function?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> function is decreasing for (–2, –1).</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> function has a minima for <em>x</em> = –1.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> function has a maxima for <em>x</em> = 2.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> function is decreasing for (–1, 2).</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Suppose <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) is a function, such that <em>f</em>′(<em>x</em>) = 8x7 f ″(<em>x</em>) = 56x6.<br />\nWhich of the following is/are true about behaviour of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) at <em>x</em> = 0?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>x</em> = 0 is a local minimum by first derivative test.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> The second derivative text fails at <em>x</em> = 0.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> The point <em>x</em> = 0 is an inflection point of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>).</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Higher order derivative is not required in this case.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/14e420b6a503066dccb01a0f55731f2e3b203da0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Find the coefficient of (<em>x</em> – 2)<sup>3</sup> in the Taylor series expansion of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = log (1 – <em>x</em>) about <em>x</em> = 2.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/23bb4a0a543042a3163819ffbb85403f56d11615.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/fc9e42a13487f0fd2f023c9eb91737da9f71d583.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/fe3d2079a31898caf71faa33c99085a58fbd6a6b.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/49c6675316e05de2ea4b4d84f062bf771edc41b2.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/bda93da21ef14e71ca6cf9b095a4276c3d6c815f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Given the function <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 15a<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 36a<sup>2</sup> <em>x</em> + 1, where a > 0, the function has a local maximum at <em>x</em> = <em>x</em><sub>1</sub> and a local minimum at <img src=\"/media/ed974032f7a7a3647cad03235d1d12896adf1f23.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Find the value of <em>a</em>.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/63fdf8b78edca78634e554cd418f708e2849b3c4.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/ab02593ef37b9e91e0741e4301f648aa16758d41.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/be9ffcba94f22541210bec4a8efaf60959166308.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/1609eb7af59b8fc4feec7ed7838b9cb59d74c484.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5354dfb3d2b92ef5a1140fd8ad48227722576617.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/cc96d0a08c4e07d2d1740e5f635d08cdc9c9dd9d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let f(z) be a complex function.<br />\nConsider the following two statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : A point z0 is a singularity of <em>f</em>(<em>z</em>) if <em>f</em>(<em>z</em>) is not analytic at z<sub>0</sub>.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : A singularity z<sub>0</sub> is called an isolated singularity if <em>f(z</em>) is analytic on the deleted disk 0 < |z – z<sub>0</sub>| < <em>r</em> for some <em>r</em> > 0.<br />\nWhich of the following is/are true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2c7ef8b88e4b9e53988fb0ce6cc387f046fac99c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":17,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T19:39:08.492Z"}